etymology_text:
Uncertain. Probably from:
* Hokkien 河 (hô, “river”), clipping from 河蠻 (“river barbarian”) or 西洱河蠻 (“Western Ear-Shaped River barbarian”), the archaic pejorative terms used to refer to people from Yunnan in the far southwest of China, recorded in the 9th century document Book on Barbarians (蠻書) by Fan Chuo (樊綽);
* Hokkien 胡 (hô͘, “barbarian”);
* Hokkien 華／华 (hôa, “China”).
Cognate with Lao ຫໍ້ (hǭ), Northern Thai ᩉᩬᩴ᩶, Khün ᩉ᩷᩶, Lü ᦠᦸᧉ (ḣoa²).

Uncertain. Perhaps from:
* Old Khmer *ʼār (“childless woman”), corresponding to Modern Khmer អារ (ʼaa, “married but childless; barren, infertile, sterile; etc”);
* clipping of อรทัย (“maiden; damsel”), which was possibly derived from Old Khmer ʼārttai (“childless woman”);
* clipping of อรไท or อ่อนไท้ (“noble woman; woman in authority; etc”); or
* corruption of อ่อน (ɔ̀ɔn, “gentle; soft; young; etc”).

