From บัว (buua, “lotus”) + ใต้ (dtâai, “under”) + น้ำ (náam, “water”); from Gautama Buddha's categorisation of persons into four types based on their intelligence:
* persons of excellent intelligence, capable of understanding things quickly, are like lotuses above the water, which will bloom immediately once hit by sunlight;
* persons of average intelligence, requiring further training or education in order to understand things better, are like lotuses at the water's edge, waiting to come above the water in the near future;
* persons of low intelligence, requiring more diligence and perseverance than usual in order to understand things, are like lotuses under the water, still having hope to come above the water;
* persons of no intelligence, yet lacking diligence and perseverance in developing themselves, are like lotuses under (or buried in) the mud, hopelessly unable to grow up to get sunlight.

Uncertain. Possibly:
* บาง (baang, a generic term for settlements located near a watercourse) + กะปิเยาะห์ (gà-bpì-yɔ́, “taqiyah”) (from Malay kopiah, from Arabic كُوفِيَّة (kūfiyya)), based on the fact that the place used to be inhabited by Malays from Kedah who produced taqiyahs for sale;
* บาง (baang, idem) + กะปิ (gà-bpì, “monkey”) (from Sanskrit कपि (kapi) or Pali kapi), based on the fact that the emblem of the district bears a picture of the Hindu monkey deity Hanuman;
* บาง (baang, idem) + กะปิ (gà-bpì, “shrimp paste”) (from Burmese ငါးပိ (nga:pi.)), based on the fact that the place used to be a location where shrimp paste was manufactured.
